{"meta": {"chapter_title": "Educational Technology Evaluation", "title": "Evaluation Questions Before Ratings", "description": "CAT 531 textbook section connecting evaluation questions before ratings to equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "course": "cat531-supplement", "chapter": "05", "section": "01", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."], "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation", "cat531_supplement_evaluation_questions_before_ratings", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "prereq_section_ids": ["cat531-supplement/04/08"], "practice_ids": ["cat531_supplement_5_1_evaluation_questions_before_ratings_artifact_signal", "cat531_supplement_5_1_evaluation_questions_before_ratings_annotation_signal", "cat531_supplement_5_1_evaluation_questions_before_ratings_ai_boundary", "cat531_supplement_5_1_evaluation_questions_before_ratings_calibration"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 1, "top_tier_hardened": false}, "content": "# Evaluation Questions Before Ratings\n\n![Evaluation Questions Before Ratings textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/05-01-evaluation-questions-before-ratings.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nStar ratings, download counts, and \"teacher favorite\" badges are answers to questions that someone else asked. A 4.7-star average tells you that a large, self-selected crowd liked something, but it does not tell you what they liked, who they were, or whether their classroom looked anything like yours. When a teacher leads with the rating, the rating silently sets the question, and the question it sets is usually \"is this popular?\" rather than \"does this serve my students?\" This section trains the reverse order: write the evaluation question first, then let it decide which numbers count.\n\nPutting the question before the rating is what turns a purchase into a defensible professional judgment. A teacher who can name the instructional purpose, the population the tool must serve, and the boundary it must not cross has built an instrument that filters evidence. A teacher who starts from the rating has only inherited someone else's filter.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Write a context-bound evaluation question that names the learner, the instructional purpose, and a non-negotiable boundary before consulting any rating.\n- Explain why an aggregate rating measures appeal across an unknown population rather than fit for a specific classroom.\n- Re-score a tool against your own question and document where a high external rating becomes irrelevant.\n- Record a before/after trace that shows the question driving the decision, including a trade-off you accepted on purpose.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA fourth-grade team needs a fluency-practice app for a class of 24 that includes 6 multilingual newcomers. One teacher opens the app store, sees 4.7 stars across 1,200 reviews, and is ready to adopt. The reviews, read closely, come overwhelmingly from parents praising the rewards animations. None mention multilingual access, and none come from a classroom resembling this one.\n\nThe decision worth recording here is not \"we picked the 4.7-star app.\" It is whether the team can state the question that the 4.7 actually answered (general appeal), distinguish it from the question they need answered (newcomer access without exposing names to a third party), and show why the two diverge.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nAn evaluation question is a sentence that fixes three things before any evidence is gathered: the **purpose** (what learning the tool must support), the **population** (the specific students who must be served), and the **boundary** (the constraint the tool may not violate, often a privacy or access rule). Once those three are fixed, the question behaves like a measuring instrument: it tells you which evidence is relevant and which is noise.\n\nA rating is the opposite kind of object. An aggregate rating is a single number compressed from many raters whose purposes, populations, and boundaries are unknown and probably mixed. Compressing all that variation into one star value discards exactly the information a teacher needs. The number is real, but it answers a question you did not ask.\n\nThe gap between the two can be made explicit:\n\n| | Evaluation question | Aggregate rating |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Who set the criterion | You, for your class | An unknown, self-selected crowd |\n| What it measures | Fit to a stated purpose and boundary | Average appeal across mixed contexts |\n| Handles your constraints | Yes, by design | Only by accident |\n| Inspectable later | Yes, the question is on the record | No, the raters' reasons are hidden |\n\nThree course anchors keep the question honest. **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)** forces the purpose clause to name a real learning demand, not a vibe (\"students practice decoding multisyllabic words,\" not \"students engage\"). **Critical edtech studies** presses on the population and boundary clauses, asking whose access the tool quietly assumes. **[AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)** asks what the tool does with student data once the activity is over, which often becomes the boundary clause itself.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nStart with a thin claim that many students write on a first pass:\n\n> I made the equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes clearer.\n\nThat claim is too thin because \"clearer\" does not identify the audience, the problem, or the evidence. A stronger claim would read:\n\n> I revised the equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes so that the intended audience can see the decision, the constraint, and the evidence source. The revision is justified by **critical edtech studies**, and I can point to the exact part of the artifact that changed.\n\nThe stronger version treats the matrix as evidence: it names what changed, ties the change to the boundary it protects, and leaves a reader able to audit the decision.\n\n### Worked Example: Writing the Question Before the Rating\n\nScenario: a fourth-grade team must choose a fluency-practice app for 6 multilingual newcomers in a class of 24.\n\n1. **Drafted rating first (rejected approach).** A teacher opens the app store, sees a 4.7-star average across 1,200 reviews, and writes \"4.7, adopt it.\" There is no record of what the 4.7 measures.\n2. **Wrote the evaluation question instead.** The team rewrote the entry as a question: \"Does this tool let a newcomer attempt a passage with first-language audio support, without exposing names to a third party?\" That question names the decision, the 6 learners, and one privacy constraint.\n3. **Re-scored against the question.** Tool A: strong on engagement, 0 of 6 newcomers could start a passage without English text on screen, so it scores low on the actual question. Tool B: plainer interface, offers Spanish and Arabic audio glossing, scores high on the question.\n4. **Annotated judgment call.** Before/after: the before-note read \"Tool A, 4.7 stars, looks fun.\" The after-note read \"Tool B selected. Tool A's 4.7 answered a different question (general appeal); our question was newcomer access, which Tool A fails. We accepted a lower engagement score to protect the access constraint.\"\n\nThe decision is now defensible because a reviewer can see the question, see why the high rating did not apply, and see which trade-off was accepted on purpose.\n\nWalk the same fourth-grade decision yourself. The branching scenario puts you in the team's seat as the 4.7-star app meets your six newcomers; each choice records a different professional judgment and its consequence.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"The 4.7-star fluency app and your six newcomers\",\"start\":\"start\",\"nodes\":{\"start\":{\"prompt\":\"Your class of 24 includes 6 multilingual newcomers. An app store listing shows 4.7 stars across 1,200 reviews. What is your first move before deciding?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Adopt now; 1,200 reviews and 4.7 stars is overwhelming evidence\",\"next\":\"adopt\",\"feedback\":\"You let the crowd set the question and skipped writing your own.\"},{\"label\":\"Write the evaluation question first: purpose, population, boundary\",\"next\":\"question\",\"feedback\":\"You fix the criterion before any number is allowed in.\"},{\"label\":\"Skim the reviews to see if people liked it\",\"next\":\"skim\",\"feedback\":\"You read the reviews, but without a question they only tell you about appeal.\"}]},\"adopt\":{\"prompt\":\"Two weeks in, your 6 newcomers cannot start a passage without English text on screen. What does the 4.7 turn out to have measured?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"General appeal from parents praising the rewards animations, not newcomer access\",\"next\":\"adopt_end\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. The rating answered a question you never asked.\"}]},\"adopt_end\":{\"prompt\":\"Lesson: leading with the rating let an unknown crowd set the question. The 4.7 measured appeal across mixed contexts, not fit for your six newcomers.\",\"feedback\":\"This is the trap the section warns against: a high rating read as high fit.\",\"choices\":[]},\"question\":{\"prompt\":\"You write: \\\"Does this tool let a newcomer attempt a passage with first-language audio support, without exposing names to a third party?\\\" Now you open the listing. None of the 4.7-star reviews mention newcomer access or data handling. What do you log the rating as?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Strong support for adoption, since 4.7 is high\",\"next\":\"question_bad\",\"feedback\":\"You let the headline number re-enter as fit evidence after all.\"},{\"label\":\"Appeal evidence about a different question; map it against your three clauses and find it silent on all three\",\"next\":\"question_end\",\"feedback\":\"You keep the rating in its place as appeal data.\"}]},\"question_bad\":{\"prompt\":\"You have re-imported the crowd question. The 4.7 still says nothing about whether a newcomer can start a passage. Re-read your own question and re-sort the rating.\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Log the 4.7 as appeal evidence, silent on population and boundary\",\"next\":\"question_end\",\"feedback\":\"Now the rating sits below your question, where it belongs.\"}]},\"question_end\":{\"prompt\":\"Lesson: with the question on the record first, the 4.7 becomes a footnote about appeal. You re-score Tool B (Spanish and Arabic audio) high on your actual question and accept its lower engagement on purpose.\",\"feedback\":\"This is the reverse order the section teaches: question first, rating last.\",\"choices\":[]},\"skim\":{\"prompt\":\"The reviews are warm but overwhelmingly from parents praising animations. Without a written question, how do you weigh them?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"I cannot weigh them yet; I need my purpose-population-boundary question first\",\"next\":\"question\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Reviews are noise until a question sorts them.\"}]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nUse the classroom value conflict first, the technology affordance second, and the published rating third. A tool choice is justified only when it answers your stated question, not the crowd's.\n\nBefore you let any number into the matrix, check that your evaluation question already exists in writing:\n\n1. **Purpose check:** Does the question name the specific learning the tool must support, in language a colleague could verify?\n2. **Population check:** Does it name the actual students at stake, especially the ones most likely to be left out?\n3. **Boundary check:** Does it name the line (privacy, access, cost) the tool may not cross, so that a high score cannot buy its way past the boundary?\n\nIf a rating cannot be mapped onto your purpose, population, and boundary, it is appeal data, not fit data, and it belongs in a footnote rather than the decision line.\n\nThe explorer below makes the divergence concrete. A 1,200-review crowd answers \"is this popular?\" while your class needs \"does this serve these students under this rule?\" Move the sliders to see how many of the high-star reviews actually touch your population and boundary, and watch the fit-relevant share collapse even as the headline rating stays high.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"How Much of a High Rating Actually Answers Your Question\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"stars\",\"label\":\"Headline aggregate rating\",\"min\":3,\"max\":5,\"step\":0.1,\"unit\":\"stars\",\"default\":4.7},{\"name\":\"reviews\",\"label\":\"Total reviews behind the rating\",\"min\":50,\"max\":3000,\"step\":50,\"unit\":\"reviews\",\"default\":1200},{\"name\":\"onpop\",\"label\":\"Reviews that mention your population (newcomer access)\",\"min\":0,\"max\":15,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"%\",\"default\":2},{\"name\":\"onbound\",\"label\":\"Reviews that mention your boundary (data handling)\",\"min\":0,\"max\":15,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"%\",\"default\":1}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Reviews touching your population\",\"unit\":\"reviews\",\"expr\":\"reviews * onpop / 100\"},{\"label\":\"Reviews touching your boundary\",\"unit\":\"reviews\",\"expr\":\"reviews * onbound / 100\"},{\"label\":\"Share of the crowd answering your actual question\",\"unit\":\"%\",\"expr\":\"(onpop + onbound) / 2\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"reviews\",\"output\":0}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\n<inline-check question=\"You raise the headline rating slider from 4.7 to 5.0 but leave the population and boundary sliders untouched. Has the rating become better evidence for your classroom?\" explanation=\"No. The fit-relevant share is driven by how many reviews touch your population and boundary, not by the star value. A perfect 5.0 from a crowd that never mentions newcomer access or data handling still answers a different question. Raising the headline number changes appeal, not fit.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Yes, a 5.0 is stronger evidence than a 4.7 for any classroom.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"No, fit depends on whether reviews touch your population and boundary, which the star value does not change.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Yes, because more stars means more reviewers checked access.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common error is treating a high rating as a high fit. Appeal and fit are different measurements, and a tool can be loved by thousands while failing the one access constraint your class cannot waive. A second error is writing the question after seeing the rating, which lets the number quietly shape the question it is supposed to be tested against.\n\nA defensible entry shows the question on the record before the rating, so a colleague can see that the evidence was filtered by your criterion and not the other way around.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nWalk a single tool through the order this section teaches. Begin by drafting the evaluation question on its own, with no app open: \"Does this tool let my 6 newcomers attempt grade-level text with first-language support, without sending names off-site?\" Notice that the sentence already contains purpose, population, and boundary, and that it could be checked by someone who has never met you.\n\nOnly now open the listing and read the rating as one more piece of evidence to be sorted by the question. The 4.7 stars get mapped against the three clauses: does any review speak to newcomer access (population)? to audio support (purpose)? to data handling (boundary)? If the answer is no on all three, the rating is appeal evidence about a different question, and you log it as such rather than as support for adoption.\n\nFinally, record the re-score. The artifact should show the tool's high external number, your question, and the line where the two part ways. That parting line, \"the 4.7 answered general appeal; our question was access,\" is the move that converts a popularity figure into a professional judgment.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: **the question** on the left, **the number it tempts you to trust** on the right. For every rating you encounter, write the question it actually answers, then write your own. The exercise makes visible how often a published number answers \"is this popular?\" while the classroom needs \"is this usable by these students under this rule?\"\n\nA strong reader of this section can answer four questions:\n\n1. What question did this rating actually answer, and for whom?\n2. What is my classroom's question, in purpose-population-boundary form?\n3. Where do the two questions diverge enough that the rating stops being evidence?\n4. What trade-off did I accept on purpose when I followed my question instead of the crowd's?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nFor this section, the matrix is strong when the evaluation question sits at the top of every row, above any score, so a mentor can see that the numbers were collected to answer your question rather than to flatter the tool. Treat the rating column as the least authoritative column. If a tool's only strength is its external rating, the matrix should say so plainly and let the question decide.\n\nA useful closing line for the artifact would sound like this: \"I changed the matrix so the evaluation question (newcomer access without third-party exposure) appears before any rating. I kept out the suggestion to lead with the 4.7-star average because that number answered general appeal, a different question from the one my class needs answered.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same tool to a high school AP class with no newcomers and reliable one-to-one devices. The purpose clause may survive (\"students practice fluent reading of dense text\"), but the population and boundary clauses change completely, and so does which rating becomes relevant. A tool that failed the fourth-grade question on newcomer access might pass here, and a glowing fourth-grade rating still would not transfer, because it answered a question about a different room. Use that shift to confirm you can rebuild the question for a new context rather than carrying over an old verdict.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/05-01-evaluation-questions-before-ratings-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"05.01\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes for CAT 531 05.01. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"A colleague says, 'A 4.7 from 1,200 reviewers must mean more than your one written question.' In your own words, explain why an aggregate rating measures appeal across an unknown population while your evaluation question measures fit for a specific classroom, and why that makes the question the more authoritative evidence for your decision.\" answer=\"An aggregate rating compresses many raters whose purposes, populations, and boundaries are unknown and mixed into a single number, so it can only report average appeal across contexts you cannot see. My evaluation question fixes the purpose, population, and boundary in advance, so it filters evidence for fit to my actual students under my actual rule. The 4.7 is real but answers 'is this popular?'; my question answers 'is this usable by these six newcomers without exposing their names?' Because only the question is on the record and inspectable, it is the more authoritative evidence even though far fewer people stand behind it.\"></self-explain>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation\",\"cat531_supplement_evaluation_questions_before_ratings\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_evaluation_questions_before_ratings\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm the matrix opens with a written evaluation question in purpose-population-boundary form, logs each rating as appeal evidence mapped against that question, and records the trade-off you accepted when your question and the rating disagreed.\n\n## References\n\n- [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160\n- [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat531_supplement_5_1_evaluation_questions_before_ratings_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_evaluation_questions_before_ratings", "stem": "A team is comparing three reading apps. Before scoring any of them, what should the evaluation brief establish first?", "options": ["A five-star rating scale so the apps can be ranked quickly.", "The evaluation question: which decision the tool must support, for which learners, under which constraint.", "A list of the most popular features each vendor advertises.", "The total number of downloads each app reports."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Ratings are only meaningful once the evaluation question names the instructional decision, the learners, and the constraint the tool must respect. Scoring before that produces numbers without a referent.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_5_1_rate_before_question", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation", "cat531_supplement_evaluation_questions_before_ratings", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_5_1_evaluation_questions_before_ratings_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_evaluation_questions_before_ratings", "stem": "Two teachers each rated a math app 4 out of 5. One had asked 'Does it help multilingual learners attempt word problems?' and the other had asked 'Is it fun?' Why are these two 4-ratings not comparable?", "options": ["They used different star icons.", "A rating only carries meaning relative to the evaluation question behind it, and the two questions target different outcomes.", "One teacher has more experience than the other.", "Four out of five is too high for any classroom tool."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A score is an answer to a specific question. Identical numbers attached to different evaluation questions cannot be averaged or compared, because they measure different things.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_5_1_ratings_are_comparable", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation", "cat531_supplement_evaluation_questions_before_ratings"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_5_1_evaluation_questions_before_ratings_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning", "stem": "You ask an AI assistant to draft evaluation questions for a new grading tool. What is the defensible way to use its output?", "options": ["Adopt the AI list as the final criteria because it is comprehensive.", "Treat each AI-suggested question as a candidate, then keep only those tied to your actual instructional decision and discard the rest.", "Skip writing questions and let the AI score the tool directly.", "Use the AI questions only if they produce a higher star rating."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "The AI can surface candidate questions, but the teacher must judge each one against the real decision and context. Unfiltered adoption imports questions that do not fit the classroom.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_5_1_ai_questions_as_final", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ai_supported_learning", "cat531_supplement_evaluation_questions_before_ratings"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_5_1_evaluation_questions_before_ratings_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision", "stem": "A principal asks why a teacher rejected a highly rated app. Which response best shows the evaluation question did its job?", "options": ["'The reviews online were mostly positive.'", "'Our question was whether it supports IEP read-aloud accommodations; it does not, so the high overall rating did not answer our question.'", "'It scored 4.7 stars in the app store.'", "'The interface looked modern and the students liked it.'"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A defensible decision points back to the evaluation question and shows the score was beside the point. The rating was high but irrelevant to the constraint that mattered.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_5_1_high_rating_overrides_question", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["evidence_based_revision", "cat531_supplement_evaluation_questions_before_ratings"]}]}, "content_version": {"content_version": "66ba822e8e10671d08ef297d7120f9b10b9a3dcc8723c23948be2ec0264cfb9c", "algorithm": "sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1", "parts": ["mdx", "meta", "practice", "misconceptions"]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "cat531_supplement_5_1_rate_before_question", "pattern": "rating_before_evaluation_question", "description": "Scoring or ranking a tool before naming the instructional decision, learners, and constraint the tool must serve", "trigger": "learner assigns stars or points without stating what the rating is supposed to answer", "feedback": "State the evaluation question first: which decision, which learners, which constraint. A rating only means something 